title: Offline subtrees
tags: [[Mount points]]
kind: reference
summary: Everything at or below a declared mount point with nothing mounted is frozen — no watch, no reconcile, never an orphan — and stays queryable.

A declared mount point is ''offline'' when its metarecord has `mfr_mount` and the path
is not a mount point right now — including when the directory itself no longer exists.
Everything at or below it is ''frozen'':

|!Component |!On an offline subtree |
|watcher |no watch there; any event landing in it is dropped |
|reconcile |pruned from the walk: nothing created, refreshed, offered or MIME-typed. A scoped reconcile aimed at or into it fails — `… is on a volume that is not mounted: nothing to reconcile until it is back` — rather than reporting "no change" |
|orphan scan and marking |"unknown", never an orphan ([[Orphan scan]]) |
|`mf_watch`, `mf_ignore` |unchanged: eligibility is evaluated as usual; the freeze is one more filter |
|queries |unaffected — a disconnected drive's metadata stays fully queryable, the whole point of recording it |

The freeze follows the ''presence'' of a mount, not its identity: a different volume
mounted there makes the subtree live — those files really are there; the mismatch is
reported by the mount status, and changes nothing else.

Plugging the volume back resumes tracking at once — the daemon follows the kernel's
mount table (`mount::follow`, as the broker does), and a mount point coming or going
under a root places the watch set again under the budget regime: an inotify watch is on
an inode, so what was watched at that path, and beneath it, is dropped and what is there
now watched; under coverage the broker marks the drive. It does ''not'' start a reconcile: its cost is unbounded on a large disk, and the volume may
have been used elsewhere for hours. Changes made while it was away are picked up by an
explicit `mf reconcile --metarecord <mount point>`, which a client should offer rather
than run behind your back.
